Hi Kishore and team,

Quick update on the disk initialization issue:

I’ve increased the EBS performance on several of the impacted servers — scaling IOPS up to ~40,000 and throughput up to ~1,500 MB/s. However, this has not significantly improved the initialization time.

The primary challenge is the size of the disks (each ~30TB). We attempted to accelerate the process using fio during our call with AWS to pre-warm/read the volumes, but the estimated time to fully process a single disk is still ~7 hours, which does not meet our recovery timeline requirements.

At this point, the only pending item is AWS approval to increase the service quota for provisioned rate across concurrent volume creation, which may help improve parallel data hydration performance. We are waiting on their response.

Given the size of these databases, restoring from snapshot alone is proving to be too slow for a true DR scenario. The client may need to consider implementing AWS DRS (Elastic Disaster Recovery) or an alternative replication-based approach for these database workloads to meet recovery objectives.





